**Finding $b$ modulo 13:**
$$-161 \equiv 31b \pmod{13}$$
- $31 = 2 \times 13 + 5 \equiv 5 \pmod{13}$
- $-161 = -13 \times 12 - 5 \equiv -5 \equiv 8 \pmod{13}$ ✓

So: $8 \equiv 5b \pmod{13}$

The inverse of 5 modulo 13: $5 \times 8 = 40 = 3 \times 13 + 1 \equiv 1 \pmod{13}$, so the inverse is 8.

Therefore: $b \equiv 8 \times 8 \equiv 64 \equiv 64 - 4 \times 13 \equiv 64 - 52 \equiv 12 \pmod{13}$.
Thus $b = 12$. ✓

**Finding $c$:**
$$-161 = 31 \times 12 + 13c$$
$$-161 = 372 + 13c$$
$$-533 = 13c$$
$$c = -41$$ ✓

**Verification:**
$$403 \times 2 + 155 \times 12 + 65 \times (-41)$$
$$= 806 + 1860 - 2665$$
$$= 2666 - 2665 = 1$$ ✓

**Answer:** $a + b = 2 + 12 = 14$ ✓

